  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 153 PM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nHot and humid conditions improve behind a cold front that passed \nthrough the region this morning. Winds behind the front are out of \nthe north and a bit breezy with gusts to around 20 mph. Dewpoints \nare falling rapidly to the low 60s. However, highs are still \nexpected to climb into the 90s and low 100s, and so Heat Index \nvalues will be 100 to 104 degrees F. A Heat Advisory remains in \neffect for areas along and south of I-90 until 8 pm CDT. \n\nIn addition to heat there is a Marginal risk (level 1 of 5) for \nstrong to severe thunderstorms. A shallow wave will pass through the \nregion, triggering storms over western South Dakota late this \nafternoon into the early evening. These will travel east and begin \nto impact areas of central South Dakota some time after 9 pm CDT. As \nstorms approach they will be encountering an increasingly stable \nenvironment. While severe weather cannot be ruled out, the area most \nlikely to see any stronger storms will be west of the James River. \nThe main risk with storms will be damaging wind gusts of 65 mph. \nStorm intensity will weaken as they continue east becoming scattered \nrain showers and weak thunderstorms. Rain accumulation will be \nlight, a trace to a hundredth for most of the CWA. West of the James \nRiver may see up to a tenth of an inch. Showers may linger into the \nmorning commute Tuesday, but impacts should be minimal. \n\nTuesday surface high pressure settles over central South Dakota \nturning winds to the southeast. Highs will be cooler, in the 80s \neast of Highway 81, and in the low 90s to the west. Dew points will \nclimb into the mid 60s to low 70s and will result in muggy \nconditions once again. However, Heat Index values will remain below \ncritical. Still, it's important to maintain proper hydration and to \ntake breaks out of the heat. Wednesday will be another quiet day \nwith highs in the 90s.  \n\nWednesday night a short wave will work through the upper ridge \nbringing chances for showers and thunderstorms. There is a very \nlarge difference in mid-range guidance on convective parameters, \ntherefore the severity of the storms is uncertain. Thursday \nisolated to scattered light showers will continue periodically \nthrough the day. Thursday afternoon a stronger upper wave moves \ninto the region. This will result in another round of showers \nand thunderstorms. Mid-range guidance continues to have a wide \nrange of solutions, and severity of storms is once again in \nquestion. Over the coming days the trends will be monitored and \nupdates issued as details become clear. \n\nAn upper level ridge will push into the region behind the wave for \nthe weekend. Partly cloudy skies and highs in the 80s to low 90s \nwith dry conditions are expected.\n\nOne final note: Smoke from wildfires in the western US and Canada is \nexpected to begin to flow into the region today. This will result in \nmilky looking skies and a decrease in air quality. You may even be \nable to smell smoke at times depending on how deeply we mix each \nday. With our current upper level pattern smoke is expected to \nremain in the forecast through at least the middle of the week. \nPeople who are sensitive to air pollutants should use precautions. \nFor the latest on Air Quality please visit AirNow.gov.",
